What:		/sys/bus/serial/drivers/oneplus-pogo-kbd/<device>/bt_address
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.3
Contact:	linux-input@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Bluetooth address of the keyboard attached to the pogo pins, as
		the keyboard sent it in its first frames, in the colon-separated
		form, most significant byte first, lower case. Reading fails
		with ENODATA while no keyboard is attached or the keyboard did
		not send its address.

		When a keyboard that sent its address attaches or detaches, the
		device sends a change uevent with these variables:

		ACCESSORY_TYPE=keyboard
		ACCESSORY_PRESENT=1 (attached) or 0 (detached)
		ACCESSORY_BT_ADDRESS=<the address above>

		While attached the keyboard's input arrives over the pogo pins;
		userspace that also connects it over Bluetooth gets each key
		twice.

		Access: Read

What:		/sys/bus/serial/drivers/oneplus-pogo-kbd/<device>/bt_name
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.3
Contact:	linux-input@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Bluetooth name of the keyboard attached to the pogo pins, the
		name it advertises, as selected by the brand it sent with its
		address, for example "OnePlus Pad 3 Keyboard". Reading fails
		with ENODATA while bt_address does, or when the brand is not
		known.

		Access: Read

What:		/sys/class/power_supply/oneplus-pogo-kbd-<device>/
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.3
Contact:	linux-input@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Battery of the keyboard attached to the pogo pins, a power
		supply of type "Battery" and scope "Device" named after the
		serial device the keyboard is bound to. It appears when an
		attached keyboard first reports its battery level and goes away
		when the keyboard detaches.

		The attributes are those of the power supply class, see
		sysfs-class-power; the driver provides present (always 1),
		capacity (the level the keyboard reported, in percent), scope
		and model_name.

What:		/sys/class/leds/<device>::micmute/
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.3
Contact:	linux-input@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Mic mute LED of the keyboard attached to the pogo pins, a LED
		class device named after the serial device the keyboard is
		bound to, with the "audio-micmute" default trigger. It exists
		while the driver is bound; its state is sent to each keyboard
		that attaches. The caps lock and mute LEDs are the keyboard
		input device's LEDs (LED_CAPSL, LED_MUTE).

		The attributes are those of the LED class, see
		sysfs-class-led.
